Optimal Solution - Greedy solution that partitions the string by expanding segments to include the last occurrence of each character.
                   Precompute each character’s last index, then greedily extend the current partition until all included characters are closed — cut only when safe.

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> partitionLabels(String s) {
        // Array to store the last occurrence index of each character (a–z)
        int[] last = new int[26];
        // Fill the last occurrence for every character in the string
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;
        }
        // Result list to store sizes of each partition
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        // maxLast keeps track of the farthest last index in the current partition
        // prevEnd stores the end index of the previous partition
        int maxLast = 0, prevEnd = -1;
        // Traverse the string to create partitions
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            // Update the farthest boundary needed for current partition
            maxLast = Math.max(maxLast, last[s.charAt(i) - 'a']);
            // If current index reaches the partition boundary
            if (i == maxLast) {
                // Add the size of the current partition
                result.add(i - prevEnd);
                // Update previous partition end
                prevEnd = i;
            }
        }
        // Return the list of partition sizes
        return result;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
